# Findings daw_lint should treat as INTENTIONAL for the projects in this directory.
# One per line: "<code>" for every scope, or "<code> <scope>" for one.
#
# This file exists so a deliberate choice can be declared once instead of being quieted
# by editing the fixture, or — worse — by nobody running the linter any more. Every
# suppression here is still COUNTED and reported in daw_lint's summary line, so it can
# be audited rather than forgotten.

# These fixtures reference `Identity`, the repo's own test plugin, by NAME with no path.
# That is what makes them portable: the Identity build product lives at a different
# absolute path in every build directory, so a path would break the fixture on every
# machine but the one that wrote it. Resolution by name is weaker than by path — it will
# silently pick a different build — and for a test fixture that is the right trade.
device-plugin-by-name-only

# NOT DECLARED HERE, and the reason is a gap in the mechanism rather than a decision.
#
# `stress-128` and `stress-512` exist to put more notes on a lane than anything is
# expected to handle, and between them they report 60,000+ note-same-tick-in-column.
# The pile IS the test, so those findings are intentional — but a declaration's scope is
# a finding's scope (`clip:2`, `track:3`), not a project, and this file covers every
# project in the directory. So the only declaration available would silence the rule for
# `maximal` and `webtest` too, and that is the rule that just found a real authoring
# mistake in both of them: two notes stacked in one column, both playing, one of them
# reachable only as a pill where two note columns would have shown both.
#
# --max-per-rule already solves the flooding (eleven lines instead of sixty thousand)
# and the count stays truthful. Left at that, and raised with backend, rather than
# trading a rule that works for a directory that looks quiet.
